# WHT exemption automation

This project answers one question for each taxpayer: does this taxpayer qualify for a withholding-tax exemption?

Uganda Revenue Authority (URA) publishes the exemption checklist to the public. That checklist is the full set of compliance checks a taxpayer must meet. This repository implements those checks as rules. A rule names one fact about the taxpayer, a comparison, and the value the check expects. How a published check becomes a rule, and how a rule is registered, activated, and enabled, is in [How a rule becomes a decision](#how-a-rule-becomes-a-decision). The five checks enabled for financial year 2026/27 are listed in [Enable the code in the cycle](#5-enable-the-code-in-the-cycle).

## Grant decision

The grant decision is the exemption answer stored for one taxpayer, identified by TIN (tax identification number). It is one of three words:

| Grant decision | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `ELIGIBLE` | Every mandatory check passes. The exemption is granted. |
| `NOT ELIGIBLE` | A mandatory check fails. The exemption is refused. |
| `NEEDS REVIEW` | A mandatory check has no usable value, so the exemption is neither granted nor refused. |

`make grant` applies the checks in force on the assessment date and writes one row per TIN. The row holds the grant decision, the result of every check, and the version of each check (`CODE:vN`). How that row is built is in [The grant evaluates every TIN](#6-the-grant-evaluates-every-tin).

## Ranker score

The ranker score is a second number stored beside the grant decision. It measures how similar a taxpayer is to taxpayers who passed the URA checklist. A high score means the taxpayer resembles those who qualified. It is not an exemption answer, and it does not change `ELIGIBLE`, `NOT ELIGIBLE`, or `NEEDS REVIEW`.

`make train` fits the model. `make score` writes a score and a High, Medium, or Low tier for every TIN. `POST /predict` returns the score for one TIN. The inputs, the three models, and those two ways of producing a score are in [Ranker score](#ranker-score).

## Lookup

A lookup reads an answer that has already been written. It does not run the checks again. `POST /grant` takes a TIN, finds that taxpayer's row in the grant table, and returns the grant decision, the evidence for every check, and the ranker score when `make score` has written one. The request and the fields it returns are in [Look up the decision](#7-look-up-the-decision).

## Which checks run

A check counts toward the grant decision only when both of the following are true.

**Activation** is a row in PostgreSQL. It records which version of a check is in force on which dates. A change, such as a higher threshold, is a new version with its own start date. An assessment before that date still uses the previous version.

**Enabling** is a line under `wht_2026_27_h1` or `wht_2026_27_h2` in `config/config.yaml`. It says that half of the financial year includes the check. The line does not set the threshold or the version. Those stay on the activated rule.

`make grant` applies a check when the half for the assessment date enables it and PostgreSQL has a version in force on that date. Activating a version stores it immediately. The grant table, and therefore a lookup, shows that version after the next `make grant` whose assessment date falls inside the version's window.

## Layout

| Path | Role |
|---|---|
| `config/` | Cycles, lake paths, Spark and serving settings |
| `src/data_engineering/` | Join seven lake tables into `wht_base_v1`, then keep one row per TIN |
| `src/rules_engine/` | Rule definitions, the features they read, PostgreSQL, the engine, migrations, and the CLI |
| `src/features/` | Leakage-safe ranker features and the Feast registry |
| `src/training/` | Random forest, logistic regression, and gradient-boosted trees |
| `src/inference/` | Batch grant table and batch ranker scores |
| `api/` | Health, grant lookup, checklist rules, and online ranker scoring |
| `feature_store/` | Feast view registered from the ranker snapshot |
| `dags/` | Daily load, features, and grant; weekly training; daily scores |

## What runs today

1. `make ingest` reads registration, ledger, payments, EFRIS, sector, customs headers, and filed returns. Registration is the spine. Each other source becomes one row per TIN. Sector duplicates keep one activity code. The checklist label (`wht_eligible`) is 1 when every mandatory rule in force passes. Review counts as not eligible yet. Four audit flags (`is_registered`, `is_existing_taxpayer`, `no_outstanding_liability`, `has_filed_returns`) are written when that rule is in the cycle and in force. A code the cycle names that has no version yet is left out of the label.
2. `make features` writes the ranker snapshot under `data/features` and registers that view in Feast. The label is the stored `wht_eligible`. Columns the checklist reads are kept out of the model inputs. Redis must be running: `make redis-up`.
3. `make materialize` copies the snapshot into Redis at `localhost:6379`. It writes 100,000 TINs per batch and prints progress. About 6.6 million TINs take roughly six minutes and 5 GB of Redis memory. `REDIS_URL` points a deployment at another Redis. `online_store.type: sqlite` in `config.yaml` keeps a local file instead.
4. `make grant` runs the rules engine over every TIN and writes `data/grants/wht_grants.parquet`. The cycle says which codes are enabled. PostgreSQL says which version of each code is in force on the assessment date. Each row has the feature behind every rule, each rule's outcome (`PASS`, `FAIL`, or `REVIEW`), the checklist status, the decision, and the versions used (`CODE:vN`). A summary with counts only is written to `artifacts/wht_grant_summary.json`, and a row is added to `checklist_runs`. `make grant` stops before Spark starts if any enabled code has no version in force on the assessment date, including a misspelled code. The table stays as written until the next grant run.
5. `make train` fits the three models. Reports contain counts, not TINs.
6. `make score` writes a similarity score and a High, Medium, or Low tier to `data/scores/wht_scores.parquet`.
7. `make serve` looks up the grant table on port 8001, serves the checklist rules API, and scores one TIN from Redis when a model is loaded.

`WHT_MODE` is `local` or `remote`. Remote reads the seven lake tables in `config.yaml`, joins them, and saves `data/delta/wht_base_v1`. Local skips the lake. Features, the grant, training, and scores read that saved table in either mode. The cluster sets `WHT_MODE=remote` in `config/airflow.env`.

`make mock-lake` writes a disposable copy of those seven tables as Delta under `mock_lake/`, on the same relative paths (`dm_modeled/dim_taxpayer_mst` and the other six). The columns are the ones the load and the feature queries read. `WHT_LAKE_ROOT` points remote mode at that directory and leaves the cluster paths in `config.yaml` unchanged. `make mock-lake-rm` deletes the directory. The Delta package is the `delta` extra.

```bash
pip install -e '.[delta]'
make mock-lake
WHT_MODE=remote WHT_LAKE_ROOT=mock_lake make ingest
make mock-lake-rm
```

The registration TINs are `1000000001` (eligible at both tax thresholds), `1000000002` (fails registration, liability, returns, and tax), `1000000003` (missing registration status, sent to review), `1000000004` (new taxpayer), `1000000005` (two registration rows), `1000000006` (registration only), and `1000000007` (passes the 100M tax check and fails the 150M check). `9999999999` appears only in a fact table, so the join leaves it out.

The daily DAG `wht_features_daily` runs at 02:00: load, then features, then Redis materialization. The grant is built from the same run, after features, and reads the taxpayer table. Training (`wht_train_weekly`) is Sunday at 04:00. Batch scores (`wht_batch_score_daily`) run at 06:00.

## Ranker score

The ranker learns which taxpayers look like the ones who passed the URA checklist. Its training label is `wht_eligible`, which is 1 only when the checklist passes. Columns the checklist itself reads are left out of the model inputs, so the score cannot copy the grant decision.

`make features` writes that snapshot under `data/features` and registers it in Feast. `make materialize` copies it into Redis, which is what `POST /predict` reads. `make train` fits three models: random forest, logistic regression, and gradient-boosted trees. The model `POST /predict` uses is the random forest named in `serving.model_name`. `make score` reads the taxpayer table and writes `data/scores/wht_scores.parquet`: a score and a High, Medium, or Low tier for every TIN. `POST /predict` scores one TIN from the Redis snapshot. A lookup can attach that score and tier to the grant decision. The grant decision stays the checklist result.

## Local run

Python 3.11, Java 17, and Docker are required. Redis and PostgreSQL run in Docker from `docker-compose.yaml`. The full Redis load needs about 5 GB free in Docker Desktop's memory limit (Settings → Resources).

```bash
python3.11 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
make install
make redis-up
make rules-db-up
make rules-db-init
make rules-migrate
make test
make serve
```

`make rules-db-init` creates the schema and leaves it empty. `make rules-migrate` loads the shipped checklist (`V001__baseline.yaml`) and activates its five rules from 1 July 2026. `make rules` prints the rules in force today.

```bash
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8001/health
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8001/ready
curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8001/grant -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"tin":"<TIN>"}'
curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8001/predict -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"entity_id":"<TIN>"}'
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/v1/checklist/rules/active
```

`/ready` stays unavailable until `make grant` has written the table. `/grant` returns 503 while the rules database is down, because the lookup rebuilds each check from the stored rule version. `/predict` needs `make materialize`, `make train`, Redis, and Java 17 in `JAVA_HOME`. The server loads the model into a single-core local Spark session at startup, which takes about six seconds; each request then takes about a quarter of a second. It returns 404 for a TIN missing from Redis and 503 when Redis or the model is not available. `make feast-ui` opens the Feast UI at `http://127.0.0.1:8888`.

## How a rule becomes a decision

The rules in this section are the compliance checks from the public URA checklist, implemented in this repository. A rule names a feature, an operator, and an expected value. It has no code of its own. The same version and the same PASS, FAIL, and REVIEW logic run for one taxpayer and for the Spark grant.

```text
feature in code
    → register a DRAFT (CLI, API, or a migration file)
    → another person may approve it
    → activate that version in the database, from a date
    → enable the code in the cycle that should apply it
    → make grant writes one row per TIN for the assessment date
    → POST /grant returns that stored decision
```

### 1. The feature already exists

Rules only name a feature. They never read raw columns. Each feature is a SELECT in `src/rules_engine/feature_migrations/`. The query reads `lakehouse.<source>` and returns `TIN_NO` plus the feature, one row per TIN. `V001__baseline.sql` holds the query in force for every feature. A later file, `V002__short_name.sql`, replaces the query for each feature it names. The latest query for that name is the one that runs. The earlier one stays on record and is not used. The file number is not the feature version: the second migration that names `tax_collected` is `tax_collected:v2`.

A count or an amount with no source rows for that TIN becomes zero. A registration value that is missing stays null, and the rule goes to REVIEW.

| Feature | Kind | Source | When the TIN has no source rows |
|---|---|---|---|
| `registration_status` | text | registration | REVIEW |
| `taxpayer_status` | text | registration | REVIEW |
| `business_age_years` | number | registration | REVIEW |
| `returns_filed_count` | number | filed returns | 0 |
| `declared_tax_total` | number | filed returns | 0 |
| `tax_collected` | number | payments | 0 |
| `payments_count` | number | payments | 0 |
| `outstanding_liability` | number | ledger | 0 |
| `waiver_received` | number | ledger | 0 |
| `efris_sales_total` | number | EFRIS | 0 |
| `efris_invoice_count` | number | EFRIS | 0 |
| `customs_red_lane_count` | number | customs | 0 |

A new calculation is the next SQL file. It names the feature, the kind, the lake table, and what a TIN with no source row becomes, then one SELECT. Editing the file that already holds the active query would keep the same version number, so the change is a new file:

```sql
-- feature: tax_collected
-- description: Total tax paid, excluding reversed payments
-- dtype: number
-- source: payments
-- missing: 0, no payments on record
SELECT
    TIN_NO,
    cast(sum(TOT_PAYMENT_AMT) - sum(reversed_tax) as double) AS tax_collected
FROM lakehouse.payments
GROUP BY TIN_NO
```

Ingest registers each source and runs that query, so `FROM lakehouse.payments` reads the payments table. A TIN the query does not return becomes 0 when the missing note starts with 0, and stays null otherwise. That file makes `tax_collected:v2` the active calculation. `tax_collected:v1` stays on record and is not used. A rule that names `tax_collected` does not change. The next ingest calculates v2, and the grant writes that version on the row. `register` refuses the rule until the feature exists and is active, and until the operator fits the feature's kind. A superseded feature cannot back a new rule.

| Operator | Expected value | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `eq`, `ne` | one value of the feature's kind | equality |
| `gt`, `gte`, `lt`, `lte` | a number | thresholds |
| `in`, `not_in` | a non-empty list of the feature's kind | membership |
| `is_true`, `is_false` | omitted | a true/false feature |

### 2. Register a draft

`make rules-db-up` then `make rules-db-init` prepares PostgreSQL at `postgresql://wht:wht@localhost:5432/wht_rules` (`rules_store` in `config.yaml`; `RULES_DB_URL` in a deployment). The CLI and the API both call the same registry.

A rule file holds one rule. The registry assigns the version, the status, the dates, and who created it. The file must not set `version`, `status`, `effective_from`, `effective_to`, `created_by`, or `approved_by`.

```bash
cat > min_payments.yaml <<'YAML'
rule_code: MIN_PAYMENTS
title: At least one payment
description: At least one payment is on record
feature_name: payments_count
operator: gt
expected_value: 0
mandatory: true
YAML
python -m rules_engine.cli register min_payments.yaml --actor alice --reason "Policy memo 12/2026"
```

`--rm` deletes the file after the draft is stored. `--actor` defaults to `WHT_ACTOR` or the login name. `--reason` is stored in the audit trail.

That prints `MIN_PAYMENTS:v1` as `DRAFT`. The same call over HTTP:

```bash
curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/v1/checklist/rules \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -H 'X-Actor: alice' \
  -H 'X-Reason: Policy memo 12/2026' \
  -d '{"rule_code":"MIN_PAYMENTS","title":"At least one payment","description":"At least one payment is on record","feature_name":"payments_count","operator":"gt","expected_value":0}'
```

A second register of the same code creates the next version (`v2`, then `v3`). A retired code cannot be registered again. Use a new code.

### 3. Approve, when a second person signs off

The person who registered a draft may activate it. Approval is optional and must be someone else. The database rejects self-approval.

```bash
python -m rules_engine.cli approve MIN_PAYMENTS 1 --actor bob --reason "Checked against the memo"
```

```bash
curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/v1/checklist/rules/MIN_PAYMENTS/versions/1/approve \
  -H 'X-Actor: bob' -H 'X-Reason: Checked against the memo'
```

The version is then `APPROVED` and still not in force. A draft can skip this step and go straight to activate.

### 4. Activate from a date

Activation puts that version in force from a date. The previous active version of the same code becomes `SUPERSEDED` and stops applying on that date. The end date is exclusive, so an assessment on the start date uses the new version, and an assessment the day before still uses the old one.

```bash
python -m rules_engine.cli activate MIN_PAYMENTS 1 --from 2026-07-01 --actor alice
```

```bash
curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/v1/checklist/rules/MIN_PAYMENTS/versions/1/activate \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -H 'X-Actor: alice' \
  -d '{"effective_from":"2026-07-01"}'
```

A start date is never moved, and a new version cannot start on or before the version it replaces. Versions of one rule never overlap, and only one is `ACTIVE`.

Raising a threshold is a new version of the same code, not an edit:

```bash
cat > min_tax_v2.yaml <<'YAML'
rule_code: MIN_TAX_COLLECTED
title: Tax collected at least 150M
description: Total tax paid is at least UGX 150,000,000
feature_name: tax_collected
operator: gte
expected_value: 150000000
YAML
python -m rules_engine.cli register min_tax_v2.yaml --actor alice --reason "Policy memo 12/2026" --rm
python -m rules_engine.cli activate MIN_TAX_COLLECTED 2 --from 2027-01-01 --actor alice
```

That command only updates the database. The engine then resolves the version from the assessment date: `MIN_TAX_COLLECTED:v1` (100,000,000) before 1 January 2027, and version 2 from that date. The grant table is whatever the last `make grant` wrote, so lookups keep returning version 1 until a grant run whose assessment date is on or after 1 January 2027. A run before that date still writes version 1, even though version 2 is already activated. The daily DAG runs `make grant` at 02:00, which is that rerun on the first morning the new version is in force. `WHT_AS_OF=2026-11-07 make grant` reruns an earlier date and writes version 1 again.

`python -m rules_engine.cli retire MIN_PAYMENTS --from 2027-07-01 --actor alice` ends the rule. It keeps applying through the day before that date, then the code cannot be used again.

### 5. Enable the code in the cycle

Enabling is the config half of the decision. Each cycle has its own list of codes, in order. A code in the list is enabled. Leaving a code out skips it. The list asks for a code. It does not set the threshold, the operator, or the version; those stay on the activated rule.

A code can be active in the database while this cycle leaves it out, and the grant skips it. A code can be enabled here before any version is in force on the assessment date, and `make grant` refuses to run. Both have to hold.

`checklist` holds the two halves of one financial year, which starts on 1 July. The keys are `wht_YYYY_YY_h1` and `wht_YYYY_YY_h2`. `YY` is the next year. The name sets the dates, so the config does not contain `start` or `end`: `h1` is 1 July to 31 December of `YYYY`, and `h2` is 1 January to 30 June of the following year. Together they cover that whole year. Any other id is rejected. Config defines `wht_2026_27_h1` (1 July to 31 December 2026, Q1 and Q2) and `wht_2026_27_h2` (1 January to 30 June 2027, Q3 and Q4). The run date picks the half and is the assessment date. `WHT_AS_OF` reruns an earlier date. `WHT_CYCLE` names a half directly, and the assessment date is held inside that half's window. A date outside this financial year stops the run until `checklist` names that year's `h1` and `h2`. Each half enables the same five codes.

`make rules-migrate` activates five rules in the database, all in force from 1 July 2026. h1 enables every one of them, so a grant inside h1 uses version 1 of each:

| Code | Feature | Test |
|---|---|---|
| `REGISTERED` | `registration_status` | equals `REGD` |
| `EXISTING_TAXPAYER` | `taxpayer_status` | equals `Existing Taxpayer` |
| `NO_OUTSTANDING_LIABILITY` | `outstanding_liability` | at most 0 |
| `RETURNS_FILED` | `returns_filed_count` | greater than 0 |
| `MIN_TAX_COLLECTED` | `tax_collected` | at least 100,000,000 |

`MIN_TAX_COLLECTED:v2` does not need a second enable inside h1. h1 already enables the code, and the assessment date still selects version 1. h2 enables the same code on its own list. A grant on or after 1 January 2027 uses h2, and the database supplies version 2 because of that date. Enabling the code in h2 does not create the version.

A new code is enabled by adding it to the cycle that should apply it:

```yaml
- MIN_PAYMENTS
```

`make grant` then requires a version of `MIN_PAYMENTS` in force on the assessment date. Until that version is activated, the grant refuses to run.

### 6. The grant evaluates every TIN

`make grant` loads the versions in force for the cycle's enabled codes, computes their features, and adds:

| Column | Meaning |
|---|---|
| one column per feature | the value the rule saw |
| `rule_<code>` | `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `REVIEW` |
| `failed_rules`, `review_rules`, `rules_failed_count` | codes with that outcome, including optional rules, and how many failed |
| `checklist_status` | `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `REVIEW` |
| `decision` | `ELIGIBLE`, `NOT ELIGIBLE`, or `NEEDS REVIEW` |
| `rules_applied` | `CODE:vN` for every rule version used |
| `features_applied` | `name:vN` for every feature those rules ran |
| `cycle`, `assessment_date` | which window decided the row |

A missing or unusable value is `REVIEW`. A value that meets the operator is `PASS`. Anything else is `FAIL`.

A mandatory `FAIL` makes the taxpayer `NOT ELIGIBLE`. Otherwise a mandatory `REVIEW` makes the taxpayer `NEEDS REVIEW`. When every mandatory rule passes, the taxpayer is `ELIGIBLE`. Set `mandatory: false` on a rule to record its outcome without changing the status. An optional failure still appears in `failed_rules`.

The grant also writes context columns (`activity_isic4_cd`, `BUSINESS_START_DT`, `REGION_NAME`, `TAX_PAYER_TYPE`) and a run row: cycle, assessment date, the rule and feature versions, and counts of eligible, not eligible, and needs review. `python -m rules_engine.cli runs` prints those runs.

The ingest label is a lighter pass over the same engine. It drops a cycle code that has no version yet, and it stores only `wht_eligible` plus the four audit flags. The grant is the decision of record, and it refuses a missing version instead of skipping it.

### 7. Look up the decision

A lookup is a read of one TIN from the grant table written by the last `make grant`. `POST /grant` loads each stored `CODE:vN` from PostgreSQL, rebuilds the check (feature, source table, operator, actual value, expected value, outcome, reason, legal reference), and checks that those outcomes still match the stored status and decision. When `make score` has written a ranker score, the response includes that score and its tier beside the grant decision. The grant decision in the response is the one stored on the row.

```bash
curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8001/grant \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"tin":"<TIN>"}'
```

A TIN missing from the table is 404. A grant table that disagrees with the rule record is 500. Rebuild it with `make grant`.

## Rule lifecycle

A version moves `DRAFT` → `ACTIVE`, then `SUPERSEDED` when a newer version takes over or `RETIRED` when the rule ends. `DRAFT` → `APPROVED` → `ACTIVE` is the same path with a second person in between. The database enforces this, so a direct SQL session gets the same answer as the CLI and the API:

- A version is never edited or deleted. A change is a new version, numbered one past the latest.
- New versions start as unapproved drafts, with no dates.
- The author may activate their own draft. The approver must be someone else.
- Versions of one rule never overlap in time, and only one is `ACTIVE`.
- A start date is never moved or backdated before the current version.
- A retired code cannot be registered again.
- Every change writes a row to `rule_audit`: action, who (`--actor` or `X-Actor`, or the database user for direct SQL), why (`--reason` or `X-Reason`), and the row before and after. `rule_audit`, `checklist_runs`, and `rule_migrations` are append-only.

```bash
python -m rules_engine.cli list --on 2026-11-07
python -m rules_engine.cli history MIN_TAX_COLLECTED
python -m rules_engine.cli audit MIN_TAX_COLLECTED
python -m rules_engine.cli runs
```

| File | Role |
|---|---|
| `src/rules_engine/rule_models.py` | Rule definition, rule result, statuses, operators, audit events |
| `src/rules_engine/validators.py` | Checks a rule against its feature; PASS, FAIL, or REVIEW for one taxpayer and the same logic as a Spark column |
| `src/rules_engine/registry.py` | Lifecycle checks, plus an in-memory registry for tests and offline runs |
| `src/rules_engine/db/` | The PostgreSQL registry (`postgres_registry.py`) and its schema (`schema.sql`) |
| `src/rules_engine/feature_computations/` | The feature registry rules validate against |
| `src/rules_engine/feature_migrations/` | SELECT for each feature over `lakehouse.<source>`. The latest migration that names a feature is active |
| `src/rules_engine/migrations/` | Ordered checklist files, applied once |
| `src/rules_engine/cli.py` | Register, approve, activate, retire, and inspect rules |
| `src/rules_engine/engine.py` | Applies the versions in force on a date to one taxpayer or to the whole table |
| `src/rules_engine/catalog.py` | Opens the rules database. It ships with no rules of its own |
| `src/rules_engine/cycles.py` | Which codes each exemption cycle switches on |

`rules_store.type: memory` (`RULES_STORE=memory`) replays `catalog.py`, which is empty unless a test passes its own changes. Tests use that registry. `tests/integration/test_rules_postgres.py` runs against a scratch `wht_rules_test` database and is skipped when PostgreSQL is not running.

## Rule migrations

`src/rules_engine/migrations/V001__baseline.yaml` is the shipped checklist. Later changes are the next file, `V002__short_name.yaml`, and are never an edit of an applied file. `make rules-migrate` applies pending files in order. Each file is one transaction: every change in it is registered and activated in the database, or nothing from that file is stored. The person who runs the command is recorded as the one who registered each rule. The file number is not a rule version.

Raising the tax collected minimum from 1 January 2027 is `src/rules_engine/migrations/V002__raise_min_tax.yaml`. Applying it activates `MIN_TAX_COLLECTED:v2`. It does not change h1's list, and it does not rewrite the grant table.

```yaml
description: Raise the tax collected minimum from 1 January 2027
changes:
  - rule_code: MIN_TAX_COLLECTED
    title: Tax collected at least 150M
    description: Total tax paid is at least UGX 150,000,000
    feature_name: tax_collected
    operator: gte
    expected_value: 150000000
    effective_from: 2027-01-01
    reason: Policy memo 12/2026
```

The same `changes` list can add a new code, which becomes `v1` of that code, or end one. A new change needs `rule_code`, `title`, `description`, `feature_name`, `operator`, `expected_value` when the operator takes one, `effective_from`, and a `reason`. `mandatory` defaults to true. `legal_reference` is optional. A retirement is `rule_code`, `effective_from` (the date it stops), `reason`, and `retire: true`. A new code still has to be enabled in the cycle that should apply it. The grant table picks up the new version on the next `make grant` whose assessment date falls in that version's window.

`python -m rules_engine.cli migrations` shows which files are applied. A database that already has rules entered by hand cannot replay `V001`. Record that file as already applied, then run later files:

```bash
python -m rules_engine.cli migrate --baseline 1
python -m rules_engine.cli migrate
```

A checksum mismatch means an applied file was edited. Add a new file instead. A fresh database runs `make rules-migrate` and starts at `V001`.

## Checklist API

`make serve` exposes the same operations as the rules CLI at `/api/v1/checklist`. A write sends the person in `X-Actor` and the reason in `X-Reason`.

| Method and path | What it does |
|---|---|
| `GET /rules/active?on=YYYY-MM-DD` | Rules in force on a date. Today when `on` is omitted |
| `GET /rules/{code}` | Every version of one rule |
| `GET /rules/{code}/versions/{version}` | One version |
| `GET /audit?code=&limit=` | Who changed what, and why |
| `GET /runs?limit=` | Grant runs and the versions they used |
| `POST /rules` | Register the next DRAFT version |
| `POST /rules/{code}/versions/{version}/approve` | Another person signs the draft off |
| `POST /rules/{code}/versions/{version}/activate` | Put a draft or an approved version in force from `effective_from` |
| `POST /rules/{code}/retire` | Stops applying on `effective_from` |

The rules database answers 503 while it is down. A refused change answers 422. A missing version answers 404. A change the database constraints reject answers 409.

## Serving routes

| Method and path | What it does |
|---|---|
| `GET /health` | Process is up |
| `GET /ready` | The grant table can answer a lookup. Includes the cycle |
| `POST /grant` | Decision, checklist, and the evidence for every rule. Optional score and tier |
| `POST /predict` | Ranker score for one TIN from Redis. `serving.model_name` chooses the model |
| `GET /models` | Models on disk and the one configured for serving |
